This paper introduces a unified framework for optimizing the sum-rate in both SISO-OFDM and MISO-OFDM systems, where the base station employs linear precoding and channels undergo block-fading. The study derives the optimal linear precoder and corresponding sum-rate for SISO-OFDM, demonstrating that the optimal solution involves allocating subcarriers to a single user and utilizing a waterfilling-like power distribution. For MISO-OFDM, the problem is decomposed into two stages: first, selecting the linear precoder and its associated subcarrier, and second, optimizing subcarrier allocation and power distribution across the remaining subcarriers. Numerical results confirm that the proposed framework yields the optimal sum-rate for both system models. Furthermore, the work analyzes the asymptotic behavior of the optimal sum-rate under high signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions, deriving closed-form expressions for the slope and intercept of the resulting slope-line. Finally, the framework's performance is evaluated against a time-division multiplexing (TDM) scheme. The findings indicate that, under low SNR conditions, the linear precoded OFDM system achieves a higher sum-rate than TDM, whereas at high SNRs, the sum-rates of both systems converge. The core contributions of this work are summarized as follows: (i) A unified framework for optimizing subcarrier allocation, linear precoding, and power distribution in SISO-OFDM and MISO-OFDM systems; (ii) Closed-form expressions for the optimal sum-rate in SISO-OFDM and a detailed description of the optimal solution for MISO-OFDM; (iii) An examination of the asymptotic behavior of the optimal sum-rate at high SNRs, including the derivation of closed-form expressions for the slope and intercept; and (iv) A comparison of the linear precoded OFDM system's performance with the TDM scheme. To address subcarrier allocation, linear precoding selection, and power distribution in MISO-OFDM systems, the following optimization model i